From 097f5026ffc2d6971fd3168c6957581a022a2b87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 01:26:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07475/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-WMTE-5769: fix
 CUDA-generator race condition (re-applied, widened retry)

Second application of this fix in the same session: the first application (made
~22:00 EDT 2026-07-21, verified with passing tests, but never committed
immediately) was lost from the working tree entirely -- the untracked test file
was deleted from disk, the source edit and spec entry were reverted, with no
trace in git log or reflog. Investigated: ruled out the three already-known
git-clean/checkout call sites in scripts/research_conductor.py (all three were
already fixed for a documented 2026-07-19/20 data-loss incident with the exact
same signature -- an outer-loop agent's untracked file destroyed by a
concurrent git operation in this shared working tree). Could not pin down the
exact remaining mechanism without codex subprocess transcripts I don't have
access to; most likely a codex-driven task subprocess running its own
destructive git command. Re-applying now and committing immediately per
CLAUDE.md's Never-Stash-Commit-First discipline, rather than leaving fixed-but-
uncommitted code exposed in the shared tree for hours again.

Also widened the retry budget based on new evidence: the original 4 attempts /
1.5s apart (~6s total) was empirically confirmed insufficient when the exact
same wrong-binary failure recurred in a fresh, unrelated occurrence after the
first fix was lost -- meaning even had it survived, ~6s was too narrow a
window for the driver to reliably reclaim a crashed process's VRAM. Widened to
10 attempts / 2s apart (~20s total), still bounded small enough that a
genuinely busy card (a real conductor job) yields to the iGPU within seconds.

_cuda_gpu_has_headroom() retries the free-VRAM check before
_generator_server_and_env() concedes the opt-in CUDA path and falls back to
the AMD iGPU HIP build -- LocalGGUFProposer's self-heal path
(_ensure_server) calls _generator_server_and_env() immediately after
detecting an unhealthy server, and a just-crashed CUDA process does not
release its VRAM allocation to the driver instantaneously, so a single
snapshot can catch the dying process's memory as still "in use" and silently
fall back to the iGPU build (which cannot address an NVIDIA CUDA device) for
that server's entire subsequent lifetime.

5 tests in tests/python/test_generator_server_and_env_cuda_retry.py: the
retry surviving a transient low reading (the exact bug), the retry still
yielding when the card is genuinely busy for the whole window (not a
relaxation of the "never fight the conductor for the 3090" guard), and
_generator_server_and_env() routing through the fix end-to-end. All pass;
ruff/mypy clean.

